<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  `overrides` — XSLT 1.0 §5.5 competing template rules. The lane RULED this type
  inapplicable; the corpus overturned that ruling.

  ## Why this type exists at all

  XSLT 1.0 §5.5 Conflict Resolution: "It is an error if a stylesheet contains more
  than one template with the same match and same import precedence." The spec then says
  what a processor does with it: the LAST such template in the stylesheet is the one
  that runs. So a second `<xsl:template match="*" mode="toc.for.section">` in one
  stylesheet SILENTLY REPLACES the first — an override in the only sense this language
  has one.

  `script/m14/ontology-coverage-gate.ts` reported the lane's RULED verdict FALSIFIED
  over the full 11,456-file admitted population: 3 sites in 3 files. The first
  counter-example it named is `libxslt/tests/docbook/fo/toc.xsl`, which declares
  `<xsl:template match="*" mode="toc.for.section">` at line 180 and again at line 190.
  Row 1 below is that file's actual shape at fixture scale.

  ## The five rows

    1. `<xsl:template match="*" mode="dup">` declared TWICE -> `overrides` from the
       LATER to the EARLIER. Both endpoints are DERIVED identities, because a
       match-based template has no `name` and this analyzer would otherwise give both of
       them the tag name `xsl:template` and collapse them into one symbol. The derived
       name carries the triple §5.5 compares PLUS an occurrence index, because the
       triple is by construction IDENTICAL for the two declarations — an identity built
       from it alone would make both endpoints the same name and the edge a self-edge.

    2. `<xsl:template match="node()" mode="uniq">` declared once -> NO edge. Without
       this row a rule that emitted an `overrides` edge for every match-based template
       would satisfy row 1 and pass.

    3. TWO NAMED templates, distinct names, SAME match/mode/priority -> `overrides`
       between the DECLARED names. When the members of a colliding group have distinct
       names, those names are written in the document and are the better endpoints.

    4. TWO templates sharing ONE name and one match -> `overrides` between DERIVED
       identities, with `metadata.declaredName` carrying the name both of them were
       written with. §5.5 makes this an error too — "more than one template with the
       same name and same import precedence" — and here the declared name cannot
       separate the two declarations, so an `overrides named -> named` self-edge is
       avoided and the derived pair is published instead.

    5. `<xsl:template name="dumptext">` declared TWICE, NEITHER with a `match` -> NO
       edge. This is the row the lane's own census probe got WRONG: it keyed on
       `match|mode|priority` and ignored `name`, so every name-only template reduced to
       the key `||` and it reported 3 "collisions" across libxslt and powershell that
       were 9 perfectly legal named templates. A named template is INVOKED by
       `xsl:call-template`; it never competes for a node, so two of them cannot
       override one another. See `validation/m14/fixtures/xml/overrides-probe.mts`.
-->
<xsl:stylesheet version="1.0" xmlns:xsl="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform">
  <xsl:template match="*" mode="dup">
    <xsl:text>row 1 first</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template match="node()" mode="uniq">
    <xsl:text>row 2 alone</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template name="winner" match="c">
    <xsl:text>row 3 first</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template name="twin" match="d">
    <xsl:text>row 4 first</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template name="dumptext">
    <xsl:text>row 5, invoked not matched</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template match="*" mode="dup">
    <xsl:text>row 1 second</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template name="loser" match="c">
    <xsl:text>row 3 second</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template name="twin" match="d">
    <xsl:text>row 4 second</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <xsl:template name="dumptext">
    <xsl:text>row 5, the other named template</xsl:text>
  </xsl:template>

  <!--
    A named template exists to be CALLED, and this call is what makes row 5's two
    declarations reachable as the same name rather than as two anonymous elements.
    It is also how the regression mutant `xml/overrides-name-only-templates-counted`
    finds the declared template names from the RESULT side: the pool pipeline strips
    custom symbol metadata, so `calls` targets are the only place a declared template
    name is recoverable after the fact. A mutant that had to guess would be a mutant
    that tested nothing.
  -->
  <xsl:template name="caller" match="e">
    <xsl:call-template name="dumptext"/>
  </xsl:template>
</xsl:stylesheet>
